# Remount the scan drive when it reappears on the bus.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# A plain /etc/fstab entry mounts once, at boot. If the device drops off the
# bus -- a nudged connector, a bus reset when another device is plugged into
# the same hub, a brief power dip -- systemd unmounts it correctly and then
# does nothing when it comes back. The mount stays dead until somebody logs in
# and notices.
#
# On a headless box with no keyboard that is the worst kind of failure: nothing
# errors, scans simply start landing on the SD card instead of the drive, and
# the only sign is one word on a 2-inch panel.
#
# the drive re-enumerated while a printer was being
# plugged into the same hub, and /mnt/scans was unmounted for good.
#
# WHY NOT x-systemd.automount
# automount would also recover, but it leaves an autofs mount on /mnt/scans
# even when no drive is present, so `is_mount()` returns true and the display
# would report removable storage that is not there. Preferring a rule that
# keeps the mount state honest over one that makes the status lie.
# Matched on the label, not the UUID, and fstab matches the same way. A UUID is
# unique but disposable -- reformatting the drive or swapping in a replacement
# changes it, and both the rule and the fstab entry would then silently stop
# matching with no error anywhere. The label is ours to set, so "the drive
# labelled share" is the contract, and a replacement prepared the same way just
# works.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_FS_LABEL}=="share", \
  RUN+="/usr/bin/systemctl --no-block start mnt-scans.mount"
